In article <1989Mar22.165854.7851@ux63.bath.ac.uk>, ma_mef@ux63.bath.ac.uk (Fletcher) writes:
> I was in a lit room with a cockatrice at the other end so I blasted
> it with my wand of fire and killed it instantly. I then hit H (shift-h) 
> to move to the left side of the room. While I was in transit I was
> blinded by a blast of light and I continued moving until I reached
> the cockatrice corpse when I automatically tried to feel what was on
> the floor, touched the corpse and died. So as soon as I pressed H I
> was effectively dead without a chance to do anything about it!
> 
> In 3.0 can the code be changed so that if *ANYTHING* happens while
> you are doing a shifted-move (H,J,K,L etc) you stop. This seems more
> sensible and in line with stopping searching or stopping opening tins
> if attacked.

This is not fair since by typing H,J, etc your intention is to move
until you find something interesting. Because you are blind, detecting
if something is interesting (e.g. whether there is something on the
floor) is done by sense, i.e. touching the floor or walls. And if there
happens to be a 'c' on the spot, well, too bad, it's a fatal error.
I think the implementation is correct as it is. Picture yourself being
blind in a hostile environment, each step you make can cause your life,
for instance, you can fall into a pool or off a cliff (so there's no cliffs
now, who knows, maybe we'll get them in a later version). So now you want
to move around blindly and savely, well, then we are back to touching
again and touching a 'c' should kill you. (unless you wear gloves (:-)).
That's the price you pay...
